From 4be31e083015e449db17154533b875f456426557 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: chuppatricky <74110004+chuppatricky@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 22:05:21 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] frame_gen: find large motion in the built-in frame
 generation

The built-in ("FSR3") frame generation searched for motion only 3 texels
around each pixel of a half-size frame, about 6 screen pixels. Turning the
camera in Ocarina of Time moves the picture 50-100 pixels per game frame at
1440p, so the search found nothing and every generated frame was the two game
frames cross-faded: a double image on every other frame while panning, seen
as a subtle flicker, and no smoother motion. A user's recording showed the
generated frames as near-exact 50/50 blends of their neighbours; rebuilding
real game frames from the frames around them scored the same as a plain
blend.

Motion is now found on a brightness pyramid of the presented frame at 1/8,
1/16 and 1/32 size: a full search of +-6 texels at 1/32 (+-192 pixels),
refined at 1/16 and 1/8 from the median coarse vectors, then a 3x3 median.
Each generated pixel tries the field's vector, the vectors of the 3x3 field
texels around it and zero, and keeps the one whose previous- and current-frame
samples agree best, so Link and the HUD, which hold still on screen, don't
smear into a panning background. On consecutive game frames from Kokiri
Forest the rebuilt middle frame's error dropped from 0.066 (blend) to 0.057;
in game the generated frames are halfway pictures, not cross-fades. Same
frame rate (36-37 FPS game, 70-73 shown, RX 7900 GRE, 1440p handheld).

EDEN_NX2_FG_DUMP_DIR=<folder> plus <folder>/fgdump.now saves the next 6 game
frames' generated frames with the game frames around them, for debugging.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit cdc0101da467b9b0d301e1cc2a3ca9c2e11affc6)
